A||N||5||SARAHS FIERO||SIMPLE CLUTCH QUESTION, IF THERE IS ONE||||1||
Z||000000||SARAHS FIERO||12-31-2001||04:24 PM||||87, 5 SPEED, 4 CYLINDER. WITH THE CLUTCH PEDAL DEPRESSED TO THE FLOOR, WHAT IS THE TRAVEL OF THE SLAVE CYLINDER ROD. ( HOW FAR DOES IT MOVE, FULL STROKE) CANT GET AN ANSWER FROM THE PONTIAC DEALER, OR ANYONE ELSE FOR THAT MATTER.<P>HISTORY-HAD A GARAGE PUT A CLUTCH MASTER AND SLAVE CYLINDER IN IT. HAD ANOTHER GARAGE PUT A CLUTCH IN IT. WORKED OK FOR 2 WEEKS AND NOW IT GRINDS 2 POUNDS OF COFFEE TRYING TO SHIFT IT AND BOTH ARE BLAMING THE OTHER. SO, ITS UP TO ME TO FIGURE IT OUT AS TO WHO IS FULL OF IT. ( IE, WHO GETS THE CAR BACK ) REASONABLY SMART ENOUGH TO UNDERSTAND HOW THE CYLINDERS WORK TOGETHER, JUST CANT GET AN ANSWER AS TO THE ACTUAL STROKE DISTANCE OF THE SLAVE CYLINDER PISTON WHEN CLUTCH PEDAL IS DEPRESSED. WHAT IS TODAYS LUCKY MEASUREMENT?<P>THANKS FOR YOUR HELP||||reg||1
Z||000001||ROB MAC||12-31-2001||04:47 PM||||I don't know exactly but it's only around 1 and 3/8 inches (approx.) It sounds like the seal on the slave is leaking. I had one go after a month. Replaced it and it has been good for 8 months. Also, the slave must be bled a certain way or you will have air bubble problems. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Joe Torma||12-31-2001||04:51 PM||||Welcome to the Forum!<P>There have been lots of people with clutch problems lately, you're not alone!<BR>I've been having a problem getting it into 2nd gear in a hurry.<BR>I don't know the travel, I have a 4speed, but there are a few other things to have them check aside from new master/slave cyinders.<P>Make sure the system is bled properly...could take hours and is probably the most common problem.<P>Bent clutch pedals(I think this is limiting the travel on my car) or banjos.  Supposidly the clutch pedal is supposed to be 1" higher than the brake pedal.  On my car it isn't.  If you look way up under there there's a rod from the clutch pedal to the master cyl.  check that for a kink.<P>Good luck!<P>------------------<BR>Its never going to be finished...<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig2_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||LZeitgeist||12-31-2001||06:06 PM||||Take a look in this thread for tips on bleeding the air out of the hydraulic line... <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/016622.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/016622.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||FieroJones||12-31-2001||06:54 PM||||An Easy fix for the bent pedal, as a tem ppatch is as follows:<P>1) Find the biggest set of waterpump pliers you can.  Mine are about 9" long<P>2) Put this on the pedal, with the arms under the pedal itself, so in essence, you've extended the pedal.<P>3) Grab the ends, grit your teeth, and pull.<P>4) When the pedal is about 1" higher then the brake, your good!<P>I've had to do this on 4 diff fiero's, and it *usually* takes 5-6 months to bend back.  I've never had a pedal break or anything, but anything is possible.   Ours went from grinding in all gears, to butter smooth.  Just a thought.   <P>Oh, also look for a steel blended pedal as well.  They still stock them at the GM Parts Houses.  They can be pricey, tho.||||reg||1
Z||000005||PK||12-31-2001||09:08 PM||||[QUOTE]Originally posted by SARAHS FIERO:<BR>[B]87, 5 SPEED, 4 CYLINDER. WITH THE CLUTCH PEDAL DEPRESSED TO THE FLOOR, WHAT IS THE TRAVEL OF THE SLAVE CYLINDER ROD. ( HOW FAR DOES IT MOVE, FULL STROKE) CANT GET AN ANSWER FROM THE PONTIAC DEALER, OR ANYONE ELSE FOR THAT MATTER.<P>Here's the info from an extract of Archies "Archism #7" : <BR> <A HREF="http://www.v8archie.com/arch7.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.v8archie.com/arch7.htm</A> <BR>A) Pontiac owners manual states that the Fiero clutch is self adjusting. In truth it is not adjustable. <BR>B) The bore size of the Master Cylinder and the Slave Cylinder is the same size. <BR>C) Through one full stroke of the clutch pedal the piston in the Master Cylinder moves 1.20" Maximum. <BR>D) Because of C) the maximum travel of the piston in the bore of the slave cylinder is 1.20" <BR>E) To properly operate the stock Fiero Clutch you need 1.15" of travel in the hydraulic system to properly engage and dis-engage the clutch. <P>There's plenty more valuable info there if you need more. Hope this helps.<P>Cheers<P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
